multimodal_crossdomain,多模态与跨域映射 (Multi-Modal & Cross-Domain Mapping),image text alignment optimal transport geometry NeurIPS 2024 2025 theory,7,NeurIPS Poster Walking the Schrödinger Bridge: A Direct Trajectory for Text-to-3D Generation,https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/116147,neurips.cc,0.9026096,"Recent advancements in optimization-based text-to-3D generation heavily rely on distilling knowledge from pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models using techniques like Score Distillation Sampling (SDS), which often introduce artifacts such as over-saturation and over-smoothing into the generated 3D assets. In this paper, we address this essential problem by formulating the generation process as learning an optimal, direct transport trajectory between the distribution of the current rendering and the desired target distribution, thereby enabling high-quality generation with smaller Classifier-free Guidance (CFG) values. At first, we theoretically establish SDS as a simplified instance of the Schrödinger Bridge framework. We prove that SDS employs the reverse process of an Schrödinger",True
manifold_riemannian,流形对齐与黎曼几何 (Manifold Alignment & Riemannian Geometry),parallel transport manifold learning representation alignment 2024 2025,7,Closing the Modality Reasoning Gap for Speech Large Language Models,https://arxiv.org/html/2601.05543v2,arxiv.org,0.89982784,"Other works employ static matching during pretraining or supervised fine-tuning. Early methods explored manifold mixup Fang et al. (2022) and contrastive objectives Cappellazzo et al. (2024) to align representation spaces. Recent LLM adaptations employ joint speech-text supervised fine-tuning Peng et al. (2025b), task-independent contrastive pretraining Züfle and Niehues (2025), or interleaving tokens directly Nguyen et al. (2025). To align output behaviors, approaches like Kimi-Audio KimiTeam et al. (2025) utilize prompt-switching, while others apply cross-modal knowledge distillation Wang et al. (2025) or data selection Cuervo et al. (2025) to match text-based teachers. Works like SSR Tan et al. (2025) combine both, aiming to align representation and output behavior simultaneously. [...] Specifically, representation alignment is computed from the cosine similarity of layer‑wise hidden states, providing coarse‑grained representation‑level feedback. Complementarily, behavior alignment is derived from the semantic consistency of the final outputs, providing token‑level but comparatively sparser feedback. These signals effectively steer the speech modality toward the text reasoning trajectory. [...] Figure 2 presents a layer-wise representation alignment analysis on the Phi-4-MM backbone, measuring the cosine similarity between the mean hidden states of correct text and speech trajectories across layers. Under this setting, similarity naturally decreases with depth due to compounding transformations, where minor modality-specific differences accumulate as they propagate through the network. The key metric is not the downward trend, but the relative separation between methods across depth. Adding RrepR\_{\text{rep}} consistently lifts the similarity trajectory across layers, indicating that representation alignment reduces the representational drift. Furthermore, our joint strategy achieves the highest similarity, suggesting that behavior alignment acts as a complementary constraint",True
