This alignment has continued to strengthen with recent advances in multimodal training methods (Xiao et al., 2025).
Consequently, when text and audio inputs share identical semantic content (e.g., audio produced as a spoken version of a text via text-to-speech conversion), their induced representations are expected to be closely aligned, particularly in the middle-to-late layers of the model. [...] More recently, omni-models have emerged as a unifying paradigm that processes and aligns multiple modalities within a single end-to-end framework (Xu et al., 2025a, b; Tong et al., 2025; Team, Meituan LongCat et al., 2025). By enabling stronger cross-modality alignment (Chen et al., 2024; LI et al., 2025), omni-models further improve multimodal understanding and support increasingly complex and interactive applications, such as voice assistants.
Accordingly, in this work, we focus on the text and audio modalities in omni-models. [...] Building upon the foundation of large language models (LLMs) (Brown et al., 2020), multimodal large language models (MLLMs) (Hurst et al., 2024; Liu et al., 2023; Chu et al., 2024; Comanici et al., 2025) extend text-centric capabilities to more modalities, including images, video, and audio.
