This section presents a novel framework for tri-modal joint representation learning that addresses the fundamental challenges of unifying text, image, and audio representations within a shared semantic space. The proposed Hierarchical Cross-modal Alignment Network (HiCAN) employs a multi-level encoding strategy that progressively aligns features across modalities while preserving modality-specific characteristics essential for high-fidelity generation. Unlike previous approaches that primarily focus on pairwise modality alignment, HiCAN explicitly models the complex interactions among all three modalities simultaneously. [...] $$\hat{x}\_{t}^{j} = x\_{t}^{j} + \mathop \sum \limits\_{{k \in M\_{tgt} ,k \ne j}} \lambda\_{j,k} \left( t \right) \cdot \Gamma\_{j,k} \left( {x\_{t}^{k} ,x\_{t}^{j} } \right)$$

where \({\lambda }\_{j,k}\left(t\right)\) is a time-dependent weighting function that controls the influence of modality \(k\) on modality \(j\), and \({\Gamma }\_{j,k}\) is a cross-modal alignment function that maps information from modality \(k\) to modality \(j\). This mutual guidance mechanism enables the model to maintain semantic consistency across simultaneously generated modalities.",False
