According to the optimal transport theory, the following p-Wasserstein distance solves the optimal transport problem: Wp ≡  min π(x,y) Z dxdy π(x, y) |x −y|p 1/p , (2.7) where the distance is the one between the distribution Q1(x) and Q2(y), providing the following constraint for the joint distribution π(x, y), Q1(x) = Z dy π(x, y), Q2(y) = Z dx π(x, y).",True
multimodal_crossdomain,多模态与跨域映射 (Multi-Modal & Cross-Domain Mapping),image text alignment optimal transport geometry NeurIPS 2024 2025 theory,9,NeurIPS Poster A Geometry-Aware Metric for Mode Collapse in Time Series Generative Models,https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/117444,neurips.cc,0.47852883,"Generative models such as Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), and diffusion models often suffer from mode collapse, failing to reproduce the full diversity of their training data. While this problem has been extensively studied in image generation, it remains largely unaddressed for time series. We introduce a formal definition of mode collapse for time series and propose DMD-GEN, a geometry-aware metric that quantifies its severity. DMD-GEN leverages Dynamic Mode Decomposition (DMD) to extract coherent temporal structures and uses Optimal Transport between DMD eigenvectors to measure discrepancies in underlying dynamics. By representing the subspaces spanned by the DMD eigenvectors as point structures on a Grassmann manifold, and comparing them via",True
coupling_barycenter_operator,"耦合与算子方法 (Coupling, Barycenters & Operator-Theoretic Methods)",Gromov-Wasserstein metric graph matching alignment ICML NeurIPS 2024 2025 theory,6,NeurIPS Poster Learning from Disjoint Views: A Contrastive Prototype Matching Network for Fully Incomplete Multi-View Clustering,https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/119585,neurips.cc,0.4629586,"a correspondence-free graph contrastive learning approach, leveraging mutual $k$-nearest neighbors (MNN) to uncover intrinsic data structures and establish initial prototypes from entirely unpaired views. Building on the prototypes, we introduce a cross-view prototype graph matching stage to resolve category misalignment and forge a unified clustering structure. Finally, guided by this alignment, we devise a prototype-aware contrastive learning mechanism to promote semantic consistency, replacing the reliance on the initial MNN-based structural similarity. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate that our method significantly outperforms various baselines and ablation variants, validating its effectiveness. [...] Skip to yearly menu bar

