## 8 Related Theoretical Research

This section briefly reviews related theoretical research closely aligned with our framework, including neural operator theory and implementations (GNO, FNO, etc.), Transformer applications in PDE solving (Transolver series), and explorations combining attention with kernel methods/PDEs. [...] Neural operators learn function-to-function mappings by introducing kernel integral operations in network layers to replace traditional neural network weight matrices, enabling learning on infinite-dimensional spaces. The original Graph Neural Operator (GNO) uses graph adjacency for integral approximation, transforming PDE solving into graph propagation learning. GNO structure includes: lifting operator 𝒫\mathcal{P} raising inputs to high dimensions, then alternately applying integral kernel 𝒦t\mathcal{K}\_{t} (parameterized via graph convolution or MLP) and nonlinearity σ\sigma, finally projection operator 𝒬\mathcal{Q} producing output. It has discretization invariance (generalizes to different resolution grids) and universal approximation theory.

